======== 7. Spin-fluid tensor from the Dirac tensor s_ijk = -e_ijkl s^l ; s_ij := s_ijk u^k ; 1/2 s_ij s^ij ========
[antisym] s_ijk totally antisymmetric  verified
[s_ij] s_ij = s_ijk u^k = [[0, 0, 0, 0], [0, 0, -cos(theta)/2, sin(phi)*sin(theta)/2], [0, cos(theta)/2, 0, -sin(theta)*cos(phi)/2], [0, -sin(phi)*sin(theta)/2, sin(theta)*cos(phi)/2, 0]]
[transverse] s_ij u^j = 0  verified (HHK condition holds for the projection)
[HHK_form] s_ij u_k == Dirac s_ijk ? False  (Dirac tensor has s_0jk, s_i0k components the HHK form lacks: the identification is a projection, a prescription)
[fluid_sq] 1/2 s_ij s^ij = 1/4
[full_Dirac_sq] s_ijk s^ijk = 3/2 = 6|s|^2 ;  (1/6) s_ijk s^ijk = 1/4
[IDENTITY] 1/2 s_ij s^ij - |s|^2 = 0 ;  1/2 s_ij s^ij + s_i s^i = 0
   => 1/2 s_ij s^ij = |s|^2 = -s_i s^i  exactly, for every orientation: the two objects have the SAME square.
      The identity is linear-algebraic in s^l, so it survives the sum over particles and both prescriptions;
      the fluid object's ensemble averages are therefore those of section 6 verbatim.
[fluid_N=2_P2] 1/2 s_ij s^ij of the summed pseudovector, averaged = 1/2  (= 2 * 1/4: True)

